WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.782
CARTER MORGAN: One of the first
problems Critter Junction had

00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:04.140
to solve was packaged delivery.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.760
Once they made a
change to code, how

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.253
would it get deployed to
users in a scalable fashion?

00:00:08.253 --> 00:00:10.170
Let's look at how Critter
Junction implemented

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.894
a gated, tag-based
CI/CD pipeline.

00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:16.282
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.255 --> 00:00:22.150
When Critter Junction
was just a prototype,

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.460
bundling up their app and
sharing it with the testing

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.343
team was fairly simple.

00:00:26.343 --> 00:00:27.760
The coders could
just email a link

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.170
to the application running
on their dev machines.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.070
But as the application and
its development team grew,

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.930
that was no longer feasible.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.180
Manually testing and deploying
changes to the codebase

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.640
became a bottleneck
for the team.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.110
Without a reliable,
mostly automatic process

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.383
to build tests and update their
microservice-based application,

00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:46.800
Critter Junction
just wasn't going

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.270
to be able to scale to
the level they dreamed of.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.820
So they implemented
a CI/CD pipeline

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.120
using Cloud Build, Google's
serverless CI/CD platform,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.630
and Spinnaker, an open-source
continuous delivery platform.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:02.110
This allowed them to rapidly
iterate and test the codebase.

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.310
The high level steps of this
pipeline are as follows.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.550
A developer changes code and
pushes it to a repository.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.520
Cloud Build detects the
changes, builds a Docker image,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.370
tests the image, and pushes
the image to artifact registry.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.850
Spinnaker detects
that image, deploys it

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.550
to a staging environment,
and tests the deployment.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.590
After all tests have
passed, the release manager

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.690
can manually approve
the build and deploys

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.280
the image to production.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.940
Let's look at each
of these steps.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.640
First, the team was
already using Git

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.260
to manage their source code.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.320
As is common in game dev, they
store binary assets elsewhere

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.480
and pull those in
when a build occurs.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.490
Using Git allows
devs to check out

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.800
a new branch of the codebase
to do their work in before

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.190
merging it back to the main
branch after it's tested.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:47.670
This lets developers easily
work with other developers

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.860
and gives you a lot of
flexibility in your workflow.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.840
Critter Junction took
advantage of Git tags

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.870
to create a CI/CD pipeline that
kicks off whenever a new tag is

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.730
pushed into the Git repository.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:01.000
They use Cloud Build to produce
application binary and set up

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.820
a trigger to watch for any
commits with a Git tag prefixed

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.470
with the letter "v."

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.480
In addition to commits
with a specific tag,

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.530
triggers can look for commits
on a branch or pull request.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.440
It then automatically
starts to build

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.190
and tests a new Docker
image and pushes it

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.595
to artifact registry--

00:02:15.595 --> 00:02:17.470
which is a single place
for your organization

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.290
to manage container images
and language packages.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.750
Check out our new series
on artifact registry

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.103
in the link below.

00:02:24.103 --> 00:02:26.020
And the final leg of the
process is kicked off

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.467
once Spinnaker detects the new
image in artifact registry.

00:02:29.467 --> 00:02:31.300
From there, Spinnaker
would deploy the image

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.160
into a scaled-down
staging environment

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.723
for integration testing.

00:02:34.723 --> 00:02:36.640
These preliminary tests
allow Critter Junction

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.910
to have the best chance
at minimizing failures.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.925
Once those tests
pass, a build engineer

00:02:41.925 --> 00:02:44.050
is allowed to flip a bit
and push the new image out

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.390
to production where Critter
Junction's audience can

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.470
access the new feature.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.680
Between Git, Cloud
Build, artifact registry,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.330
and Spinnaker,
Critter Junction was

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.840
able to develop an automatic,
gated, tag-based, continuous

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.940
integration and
deployment pipeline that

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.520
allowed for faster
iterations, more flexibility,

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.557
and reliability for their
containerized environment.

00:03:03.557 --> 00:03:05.140
This pipeline allowed
Critter Junction

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.030
to smoothly scale
up and quickly add

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.160
new features as their
game gained more audience

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.060
and acclaim.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.400
Check out the link below
to learn more, and thanks

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.230
for joining us for
season three of season

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.690
of scale on microservices,
statelessness,

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.290
and continuous delivery.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.290
Stay tuned for what's
next for Critter Junction.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.950
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.300
